---
layout: base.njk
title: Get started
permalink: /get-started/
description: Run telos with Docker Compose, sign in, and start adding todos. The board and agents are optional.
---
<section class="page-hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">Get started</p>
  <h1>From nothing to your first todo in a few minutes</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Telos runs as one container plus Postgres. Nothing to clone and nothing to
    build: save a compose file, make two secrets, start it. After step 4 you
    have the whole todo app. Steps 5 and 6 are extras you can skip.
  </p>
</section>

<div class="steps">
  <section class="step">
    <h2>Save the compose file</h2>
    <p>Make an empty directory and save this in it as <code>docker-compose.yml</code>:</p>
    <pre class="code"><code>name: telos

services:
  telos:
    image: vantreeseba/telos:latest
    restart: unless-stopped
    depends_on:
      postgres:
        condition: service_healthy
    environment:
      AUTH_SECRET: ${AUTH_SECRET:?generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`}
      DATABASE_URL: postgres://telos:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?set a database password}@postgres:5432/telos
      <span class="cmt"># The address you actually reach Telos at. Magic-link URLs are built from it.</span>
      APP_URL: ${APP_URL:-http://localhost:3001}
      NODE_ENV: production
    ports:
      - "${PORT:-3001}:3001"

  postgres:
    image: postgres:17-alpine
    restart: unless-stopped
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: telos
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?set a database password}
      POSTGRES_DB: telos
    volumes:
      - telos_pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U telos -d telos"]
      interval: 5s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 10

volumes:
  telos_pgdata:</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <h2>Make the secrets and start it</h2>
    <p>It refuses to start without both, so generate them into <code>.env</code> beside the compose file:</p>
    <pre class="code"><code>printf 'AUTH_SECRET=%s\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=%s\n' \
  "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" "$(openssl rand -hex 24)" &gt; .env

docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Migrations run at boot, so there is no setup step. Keep that
      <code>.env</code>: <code>AUTH_SECRET</code> signs sessions and
      <code>POSTGRES_PASSWORD</code> is the database's own.
    </p>
    <div class="gs-callout">
      <strong>Upgrading</strong> is <code>docker compose pull &amp;&amp; docker compose up -d</code>.
      Your data lives in the <code>telos_pgdata</code> volume, which survives
      <code>docker compose down</code>.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <h2>Sign in</h2>
    <p>
      Open <code>http://localhost:3001</code> and sign in with any email
      address. Telos ships no mail provider, so the magic link goes to the log:
    </p>
    <pre class="code"><code>docker compose logs -f telos</code></pre>
    <p>The first account to sign up is the admin.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <h2>Make a project and add todos</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>New project</strong> in the sidebar, then type a todo in a
      sentence and press Enter. Tick it off when it is done. From there:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Set what a todo waits on, and it shows as <strong>blocked</strong>, with its blockers named, until they are done.</li>
      <li>Give todos labels and due dates. A late one is flagged, and the project can sort by due date.</li>
      <li>Press <code>n</code> to add, <code>/</code> to filter, and <code>Ctrl K</code> to search every project.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="gs-callout">
      <strong>That is the app.</strong> If a list of todos is all you want,
      you can stop here. The rest of this page is extras.
    </div>
    <h3>The board <span class="pill">optional</span></h3>
    <p>
      The same todos as lanes you name yourself, one of which means done.
      Switch to <strong>Board</strong> on the project. Dragging a card into
      the done lane ticks it off, and ticking it moves the card.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <h2>Put an agent on a lane <span class="pill">optional</span></h2>
    <p>AI is off at every level until you turn it on.</p>
    <h3>Switch it on</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Instance</strong>: as the admin, Settings → AI → "AI on this instance".</li>
      <li><strong>Account</strong>: Settings → AI. Settings gains the Agents and MCP servers tabs.</li>
      <li><strong>Project</strong>: its AI switch, and under it <strong>auto-run</strong> if you want agents to start on todos by themselves.</li>
    </ol>
    <h3>Make an agent</h3>
    <p>
      Settings → Agents → <strong>Start from</strong> one of the four starters:
      a <strong>Refiner</strong> for drafts, a <strong>Planner</strong> that
      splits work into todos, a <strong>Worker</strong> that does it and a
      <strong>Reviewer</strong> that rules PASS or FAIL. Type the endpoint's
      base URL (any OpenAI-compatible one, a local Ollama included) and pick a
      model from the list it returns.
    </p>
    <h3>Give it a lane</h3>
    <p>
      On the board, open the lane's menu → <strong>Agent…</strong>, and set
      where a passing run and a failing run send the todo. With auto-run off,
      press <strong>Run now</strong> on a card to work it once.
    </p>
    <div class="gs-callout">
      The app keeps a <strong>setup checklist</strong> on the home page until
      every step for a first run is taken, and each row links to where it is
      fixed.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <h2>Connect your own client <span class="pill">optional</span></h2>
    <p>
      With AI on, make an API key in Settings and point any MCP client, Claude
      Code for one, at <code>/mcp</code> on your instance. Each key has a switch
      per tool, all on until you turn one off.
    </p>
    <pre class="code"><code><span class="cmt"># Claude Code, for example</span>
claude mcp add --transport http telos http://localhost:3001/mcp \
  --header "x-api-key: &lt;your API key&gt;"</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="step">
    <h2>Before you put it on a domain</h2>
    <p>
      Registration is open: any address that completes a sign-in gets an
      account. Put Telos behind a reverse proxy with TLS, and read
      <a href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}#expose">Before you expose it</a> first.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>

